00:05:45.249 --> 00:05:50.199
The proposal dinner with Mariano and Isabela
is an absolute comedic gem as every thread

00:05:50.199 --> 00:05:53.270
in the story collides to spectacular effect.

00:05:53.270 --> 00:05:58.729
Mirabel watches in agony as Dolores begins
to reveal the secret about Bruno's vision.

00:05:58.729 --> 00:06:03.129
And the reactions of each family member as
the story passes along the line builds both

00:06:03.129 --> 00:06:05.749
the laughs and the sense of impending doom.

00:06:05.749 --> 00:06:09.680
And her antics as she attempts to cover up
the house literally splitting apart...

00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:10.819
You're doing great.

00:06:10.819 --> 00:06:13.219
...draw looks that could kill from Isabela.

00:06:13.219 --> 00:06:17.810
As if that wasn't enough, one of Antonio's
coatis steals a piece of Bruno's vision and

00:06:17.810 --> 00:06:22.759
Luisa breaks down as she struggles to move
the piano.

00:06:22.759 --> 00:06:25.699
Chaos descends and Dolores blurts out:
Mirabel found Bruno's vision.

00:06:25.699 --> 00:06:26.699
She's in it.

00:06:26.699 --> 00:06:29.789
She's gonna destroy the magic and now, we're
all doomed!

00:06:29.789 --> 00:06:34.139
The house cracks and one of Isabela's flowers
sprouts out of the floor and sucker-punches

00:06:34.139 --> 00:06:39.229
Mariano in the face, foreshadowing her reveal
later that she doesn't really want to marry

00:06:39.229 --> 00:06:40.229
him.

00:06:40.229 --> 00:06:45.710
And to top it all off, the townspeople pop
up with their laughably well-timed congratulations.

00:06:45.710 --> 00:06:47.160
¡Felicidades!

00:06:47.160 --> 00:06:53.039
After Bruno's rat pack lead Mirabel to find
where her uncle's been hiding all these years,

00:06:53.039 --> 00:06:57.240
we get some disgustingly funny moments thanks
to his little rodent friends,

00:06:57.240 --> 00:07:01.499
who use his hairbrush and his toothbrushes
to clean themselves, run the old-fashioned

00:07:01.499 --> 00:07:06.839
record player using it like an exercise wheel,
fight over food scraps in his trousers, and

00:07:06.839 --> 00:07:08.129
provide free entertainment.

00:07:08.129 --> 00:07:09.569
What do you like?

00:07:09.569 --> 00:07:10.569
Sports?

00:07:10.569 --> 00:07:14.639
This scene is a sneaky reference to Colombia's
historic match against West Germany in the

00:07:14.639 --> 00:07:20.259
1990 World Cup and there's even a nod to "El
Cole", Colombia's most colourful best-known

00:07:20.259 --> 00:07:21.319
football fan.

00:07:21.319 --> 00:07:24.940
And the telenovela plot about a forbidden
love involving an aunt...

00:07:24.940 --> 00:07:28.789
Their love could never be.
...is a shout-out to Gabriel García Márquez's

00:07:28.789 --> 00:07:32.919
novel One Hundred Years of Solitude which
heavily influenced Encanto.

00:07:32.919 --> 00:07:36.080
Hat-tip to everyone in my video comments who
spotted these!

00:07:36.080 --> 00:07:38.720
The rats also reveal Bruno's whereabouts to
Dolores...

00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:40.960
The rats talking in the walls.

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:44.369
...and to Antonio:
The rats told me everything.

00:07:44.369 --> 00:07:45.929
Don't eat those.

00:07:45.929 --> 00:07:48.539
(GROWLS)
And the little rodents put in another appearance

00:07:48.539 --> 00:07:51.559
at the end of the movie as the family's photographers!

00:07:51.559 --> 00:07:56.349
Mirabel's horrified reaction when she finds
out that to save the miracle she has to make

00:07:56.349 --> 00:07:58.580
peace with Isabela is just perfect.

00:07:58.580 --> 00:08:02.210
(GROWLS)
Look at how reluctant Mirabel is and how she

00:08:02.210 --> 00:08:05.189
tries to get it over with as quickly as possible...

00:08:05.189 --> 00:08:07.360
So, we should just hug.

00:08:07.360 --> 00:08:09.110
Let's hug it out.

00:08:09.110 --> 00:08:14.199
compared to her sister who drags it out, gloatingly
demanding an apology for the ruined dinner

00:08:14.199 --> 00:08:15.389
with Mariano.

00:08:15.389 --> 00:08:17.199
Go on.

00:08:17.199 --> 00:08:18.199
Apologize.

00:08:18.199 --> 00:08:21.910
Mirabel just can't go through with it though
and her accusations...

00:08:21.910 --> 00:08:22.910
you selfish...

00:08:22.910 --> 00:08:24.349
(GASPS)
...entitled princess!

00:08:24.349 --> 00:08:27.099
...finally get Isabela to reveal her true
feelings.

00:08:27.099 --> 00:08:31.990
I chuckled as she makes flowers sprout up
into Mirabel's face as her anger at having

00:08:31.990 --> 00:08:34.270
to be constantly perfect erupts.

00:08:34.270 --> 00:08:38.490
As for her song, there's definite shades of
Elsa's "Let It Go" song about it, which is

00:08:38.490 --> 00:08:44.649
funny given the Elsa-like poses in these flower
statues, one of which Isabela goes on to decapitate.

00:08:44.649 --> 00:08:50.579
The multi-coloured world Isabela creates for
herself, embracing vibrant and sharply-contrasting

00:08:50.579 --> 00:08:55.210
colours, is just gorgeous and a world away
from her previous pastel colours.

00:08:55.210 --> 00:08:59.620
There's even a nice edge to her song as she
grows some canivorous plants.

00:08:59.620 --> 00:09:04.220
And it's impossible not to laugh when Isa
makes another flower pop up out of nowhere

00:09:04.220 --> 00:09:07.160
to bop Mariano in the nose again!

00:09:07.160 --> 00:09:11.870
Luisa's burn-out anthem, 'Surface Pressure',
is an insanely catchy, cleverly thought-out

00:09:11.870 --> 00:09:16.899
piece of storytelling by Lin-Manuel Miranda
about the burden of impossible expectations.

00:09:16.899 --> 00:09:22.880
And the whole sequence is beautifully animated,
with loads of entertaining moments and ingenious

00:09:22.880 --> 00:09:26.240
hidden details,
from the Titanic-style ship with the Madrigal

00:09:26.240 --> 00:09:31.410
name on the side heading for an iceberg, to
Luisa shooting into the sky in a superhero

00:09:31.410 --> 00:09:35.840
pose, to Mirabel desperately holding on to
a seemingly storm-tossed ship, but when the

00:09:35.840 --> 00:09:40.910
camera pulls back we see she's actually riding
the waves of Luisa's bulging bicep.

00:09:40.910 --> 00:09:42.990
And then there's the donkeys!

00:09:42.990 --> 00:09:43.990
Everywhere!

00:09:43.990 --> 00:09:48.350
They're Luisa's backing dancers, and get some
fun Busby Berkeley-style shots during the

00:09:48.350 --> 00:09:51.279
song;
they're the band playing on the Madrigal ship;

00:09:51.279 --> 00:09:55.560
they're the audience in the battle with Cerberus;
they weigh down Luisa's scales;

00:09:55.560 --> 00:09:59.800
and they're decked out like unicorns with
pink eye shadow to match the candy-coloured

00:09:59.800 --> 00:10:03.890
clouds as Luisa rides them through the sky
in a rare moment of joy.

00:10:03.890 --> 00:10:08.819
Then there's this moment where they slow-mo
fall through the air, which I just love.

00:10:08.819 --> 00:10:13.149
And one's even there at the end of the movie
with a tray of drinks to help Luisa finally

00:10:13.149 --> 00:10:14.149
relax!

00:10:14.149 --> 00:10:18.690
And if you want to discover even more brilliant
details and Disney easter eggs hidden in Encanto,

00:10:18.690 --> 00:10:21.050
tap here to watch my Things You Missed video.

00:10:21.050 --> 00:10:22.899
There's also a link in the video description.

00:10:22.899 --> 00:10:25.830
So what are your favourite moments in Encanto?

00:10:25.830 --> 00:10:30.019
Let me know in the comments below and if you
enjoyed this, then do like and share this

00:10:30.019 --> 00:10:32.160
video;
I really appreciate it.

00:10:32.160 --> 00:10:36.480
You can also tap left to learn all about the
incredible deleted scenes and characters cut

00:10:36.480 --> 00:10:40.170
from the final movie, such as Isabela's magical
potato people.

00:10:40.170 --> 00:10:42.310
Thanks for watching and see ya next time.

00:10:42.310 --> 00:10:43.790
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers!

